# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-cad-load-error-message-says-only-error — a CAD-side failure is reported without naming its cause
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

verify:epure-api:windows on master (alfe 2.2.96, job 16753534728), the
BricsCAD run that loads pjb's two expressions from a file. The failure
is now REPORTED (that is alfe-eval-x-output-lost-on-cad, fixed in
2.2.91) -- but what it says is:

#+begin_example
protocol: status now "DONE 1 FAIL" (elapsed 0.06 s)
ERROR protocol request 1: while loading C:\msys64\tmp\alfe-epure-api.lsp
  (form starting at line 1) at line 1, column 1: error
ERROR protocol request 1: while loading C:\msys64\tmp\alfe-epure-api.lsp
  (form starting at line 1) at line 1, column 1: error
cli: plan finished status=:FAILED exit=1
#+end_example

The cause is the single word =error=. The file's first form is
=(print (= "" (toutes_options nil)))=, and the message does not say
whether TOUTES_OPTIONS is undefined, was called with the wrong argument
count, or raised something itself -- which is exactly what the user
needs in order to act. "at line 1, column 1" is the position of the
whole form, so it does not narrow it down either.

(The duplicate line is a separate, already-filed defect:
alfe-cad-error-reported-twice-on-stderr.)

* Questions to settle

1. Where does the word =error= come from? Candidates: BricsCAD's own
   =*error*= message text for this condition; the runtime's
   =autolisp-effective-error-message= falling back to a placeholder when
   the message is empty; or =autolisp-source-load='s wrapper discarding
   the inner message while keeping its own frame.
2. Does a DIRECT =-x= of the same form give a better message? The eval
   path and the load path reach the error differently
   (=autolisp-eval-load-form= versus the alfe-eval.lsp load), and the
   comparison localises the loss.
3. Does the CAD have more to say elsewhere -- =protocol/stderr.txt= raw,
   the session log, =*AUTOLISP_ERROR_MSG*= -- that the report drops?

The clautolisp-hosted harness added in
alfe-autocad-shadow-path-spurious-newlines answers 1 and 2 without a
CAD: it can load a file whose form signals and show exactly what the
runtime captures. What it cannot answer is what BricsCAD itself puts in
the message; that needs the Windows runner.

* Acceptance

- A CAD-side failure names its cause: the engine's own message text, or
  an explicit statement that the engine supplied none.
- A regression test pins the message for a form that signals in the
  hosted harness.

* Resolved — clautolisp 2.2.104

Question 1 answered: the word is BRICSCAD'S. This runtime does not lose
the text. The same two shapes, hosted under clautolisp, report it in
full:

#+begin_example
;; from a FILE
ERROR protocol request 1: while loading …/api.lsp (form starting at
  line 1) at line 1, column 1: Undefined AutoLISP function TOUTES_OPTIONS.
;; as a direct form
ERROR protocol request 2: Undefined AutoLISP function TOUTES_OPTIONS.
#+end_example

So question 2 is answered too -- both paths carry it -- and question 3
does not arise: nothing was dropped on the way. What pjb saw is what
BricsCAD supplied for that condition: the bare word =error=.

What can still be done is make such a report USABLE, and that is what
ships:

- =autolisp-vague-error-message-p= recognises a message that says
  nothing: absent, empty, or one of the bare words a host offers
  (=error=, =erreur=, =Function cancelled=).
- =autolisp-request-failure-text= then appends the FORM -- the one piece
  of context this side always has -- and says plainly when the engine
  gave no text at all:

#+begin_example
ERROR protocol request 1: the engine supplied no message [form: (ERROR "")]
ERROR protocol request 2: error -- the engine said no more [form: (ERROR "error")]
ERROR protocol request 3: Undefined AutoLISP function NO_SUCH_FUNCTION.
#+end_example

  An informative message is passed through untouched, with no form
  appended, so nothing gets noisier.
- =autolisp-raise= no longer lets an EMPTY message fall through to the
  arithmetic it raises with: an empty error context lost to the host's
  "divide by zero", so a user was told what this runtime did rather than
  what happened. It now records "the engine supplied no message", which
  the loop recognises as vague and completes with the form.

Test: =protocol-a-vague-engine-message-gains-the-form= (hosted under
clautolisp) covers all three shapes. alfe suite 1200 checks, 0 failures.

On BricsCAD pjb's report will now read
=…: error -- the engine said no more [form: (PRINT (= "" (TOUTES_OPTIONS NIL)))]=,
which names the call that failed. Which of its parts BricsCAD objects to
is a question for that engine; the report no longer hides it.
